\ Spitfire: the aircraft types the player flies, each a frame of figures
\ that flight.telic's forces and moments read. A type gives its loaded
\ mass, wing, engine, drag polar and lift; its tail's areas and arms; the
\ centre of gravity's margin ahead of the neutral point; the ailerons' lift,
\ how stiff each control is to move against the air, and the speed at
\ which the wing's twist reverses the ailerons; its masses, each at a
\ place on the model, from which the moments of inertia are summed; and
\ its wheels, the main wheels' and the tailwheel's centres and radii as the
\ models in aircraft.telic draw them, on which it rests and rolls; the
\ propeller's hub, about which its 1.64 m blades turn; and the bottom of
\ its fuselage, the models' lowest ring, on which it lands with the
\ wheels up.
\
\ The Spitfire Mk I's published figures: the loaded weight, 2,624 kg; the
\ wing, 11.23 m and 22.48 m²; the Merlin III, 1,030 hp and 1,335 lb; the
\ Brownings, 21 lb 14 oz each; the dihedral, 6°; the centre of gravity's
\ permissible range, 2.7% of the mean chord; the steady rates of roll of
\ fabric-covered Frise ailerons, 92°/s at 168 mph, 75 at 200, 56 at 250,
\ 40 at 300 and 21 at 378, and the Mk I's 14°/s at 400 mph (Flight, 26
\ December 1946, from J. Smith's paper to the Royal Aeronautical Society);
\ the stick force, about 5 lb a g, is a later Spitfire's (the
\ Mk XVI's) taken for the Mk I. Measured from the model: the mean chord,
\ 2.165 m; the wing's root, 0.70 m below the propeller's axis; the tailplane, 2.89 m², its quarter chord 4.25 m behind the
\ model's origin; the fin and rudder, 1.47 m² of the 1.84 m² outline
\ standing clear of the fuselage, centred 4.6 m behind, from the
\ fuselage's top there, 0.33 m above the propeller's axis, to the fin's tip,
\ 1.25 m above it; their arms from the
\ centre of gravity at its published place, 1.48 m ahead of the origin. Estimates: the margin, 3% of the chord; the
\ tail's and fin's lift slopes, efficiency and the downwash; the
\ elevator's and rudder's effectiveness and travel; the flaps' pitching
\ moment; the pilot's greatest force on the stick sideways (50 lb), fore
\ and aft (30 lb) and on the rudder bar (150 lb); the masses not named as
\ published and where every mass sits. The ailerons' lift, their stiffness
\ and the speed at which the wing's twist would reverse them are fitted to
\ those measured rates of roll: 480 mph, where the paper gives 580 mph as
\ the earlier wings' theoretical reversal speed.

\ the tail's lift slope, per radian; the fin's; the share of the free stream's dynamic pressure at the tail; the downwash, as a share of the angle of attack; the elevator's effectiveness, the share of a whole surface's lift a deflection gives (the rudder's comes from each type's rudder chord share); their travel, radians; the fuselage's yawing moment against the fin's, per radian of sideslip; the allowance for the downwash's lag in pitch damping; all estimates
3.5 constant tail-lift-slope
3.0 constant fin-lift-slope
0.9 constant tail-efficiency
0.45 constant downwash
0.45 constant elevator-effectiveness
0.436 constant elevator-travel
0.436 constant rudder-travel
-0.02 constant fuselage-yaw
1.45 constant pitch-lag

\ a flap's effectiveness, the share of a whole surface's lift a deflection gives, from the flap's share of the chord: thin-airfoil theory's 1 − (θ − sin θ)/π with cos θ = 2 × share − 1, none for no flap and all for the whole surface; 0.66 at the Spitfire's 0.31
: flap-effectiveness ( share -- effectiveness )
	| share |
	share 2 * 1 - acos to theta
	1 theta theta sin - PI / - ;

\ the pilot's greatest forces, lb: the stick sideways, fore and aft, the rudder bar (estimates); the airspeed, m/s, up to which full rudder takes no more than the greatest force (an estimate, 200 mph)
50 constant aileron-force
30 constant elevator-force
150 constant rudder-force
89.4 constant full-rudder-speed

\ the Brownings' rounds a minute, for a type's seconds of fire
1150 constant browning-rate

\ the air's dynamic pressure at sea level for an airspeed, Pa
: sea-level-pressure ( speed -- pascals ) sq 0.6125 * ;

\ a mass at a place on the model, [ out up along ] metres, its own radii of gyration about the three axes, and whether it is fuel, burned away as the tanks empty
: component ( mass place spread fuel? -- component )
	| mass place spread fuel? |
	{ :mass mass :place place :spread spread :fuel fuel? } ;

\ components at points
: point-mass ( mass place -- component ) [ 0 0 0 ] false component ;
: fuel-mass-at ( mass place -- component ) [ 0 0 0 ] true component ;

\ the centre of gravity of components, [ out up along ]
: centre-of-gravity ( components -- point )
	| components |
	components ( part | part@mass ) map 0 ' + reduce to total
	components ( part | part@place part@mass v* ) map [ 0 0 0 ] ' v+ reduce total 1 swap / v* ;

\ the moments of inertia of components about a point, [ roll pitch yaw ] kg m²: about the line of flight, the span and the vertical
: moments-of-inertia ( components centre -- inertia )
	| components centre |
	components ( part |
		part@place centre v- spread to along to up to out
		part@spread spread to spread-along to spread-up to spread-out
		part@mass to m
		[ out sq up sq + spread-out sq + spread-up sq + m *
		  up sq along sq + spread-up sq + spread-along sq + m *
		  out sq along sq + spread-out sq + spread-along sq + m * ]
	) map [ 0 0 0 ] ' v+ reduce ;

\ the figures the Spitfire and the Hurricane share, which a type gives only where it differs: the propeller's 1.64 m radius and the Merlin's 0.477 reduction gear, turning clockwise seen from the cockpit (:rotation 1; -1 the other way); the Merlin III's rated 3,000 rpm, its +6 lb rated boost and +12 lb with the boost cut-out (published); the two-pitch propeller's revolutions at full throttle standing still, fine and coarse; the fuel burned at full power, 86 gallons an hour, from an assumed 0.6 lb a horsepower-hour; a liquid-cooled engine: the glycol's heating at full power over the cooling's coefficient, °C × m/s, and its capacity over that coefficient, seconds × m/s, its published 120°C limit and the 135°C at which it boils away, the oil's temperature 0.75 of the glycol's; the split flaps' added lift, 0.35, worked by their selector; the trim tab holding 0.4 of the elevator's travel; the ailerons' rigging, the share of their travel the riggers' fixed tab holds them at with the stick centred, right positive, none where the wing roots' swirl already carries the torque; a retractable undercarriage (estimates where not published)
{ :propeller-radius 1.64 :propeller-gear 0.477 :rotation 1 :aileron-rigging 0 :rated-revolutions 3000 :rated-boost 6 :emergency-boost 12 :fine-revolutions 2650 :coarse-revolutions 2100 :full-power-flow 86
  :air-cooled false :engine-heat 12400 :cooling-capacity 11160 :temperature-limit 120 :temperature-seizing 135 :oil-share 0.75
  :flap-lift 0.35 :automatic-flaps false :slat-open-angle 0 :slat-full-angle 1 :trim-range 0.4 :trim-rate 0.4 :fixed-undercarriage false } constant merlin-figures

\ a type from its figures, over the Merlin types' where it gives none of its own: the centre of gravity and the moments of inertia dry and of the fuel when full; the tail's and fin's volumes; the pitching moment's terms, the wing's lift about the centre of gravity, the tail's restoring slope, the offset that trims at the trim lift, the damping and the elevator's power; the yawing moment's terms, the fin's stability and the fuselage's against the sideslip, the fin's damping and the rudder's power, its effectiveness from the rudder's share of the fin's chord; each control's stiffness, lb of stick force at full deflection for each pascal of dynamic pressure, the elevator's from the stick force for each g of a steady pull-up at sea level, whose elevator holds both the extra lift against the margin and the pitch damping of the pull's rotation; the dynamic pressure at the ailerons' reversal; the arm of each wing half's lift, the centroid of a half ellipse; the share of the weight the tailwheel carries at rest, from the centre of gravity's place between it and the main wheels
: aircraft-type ( figures -- type )
	| figures |
	merlin-figures figures merge to figures
	figures@components to parts
	parts centre-of-gravity to centre
	parts ( part | part@fuel not ) filter centre moments-of-inertia to dry-inertia
	parts ( part | part@fuel ) filter centre moments-of-inertia to fuel-inertia
	figures@wing-area to area
	figures@chord to chord
	figures@span to span
	figures@tailplane-area figures@tail-arm * area chord * / to tail-volume
	figures@fin-area figures@fin-arm * area span * / to fin-volume
	tail-volume tail-efficiency * tail-lift-slope * 1 downwash - * to tail-slope
	tail-slope figures@lift-slope / figures@static-margin - to wing-arm
	figures@trim-lift figures@zero-lift-coefficient - figures@lift-slope / to trim-angle
	tail-slope trim-angle * wing-arm figures@trim-lift * - to pitch-offset
	-2 tail-volume * tail-efficiency * tail-lift-slope * figures@tail-arm chord / * pitch-lag * to pitch-damping
	tail-volume tail-efficiency * tail-lift-slope * elevator-effectiveness * elevator-travel * to elevator-power
	fin-volume tail-efficiency * fin-lift-slope * to fin-stability
	-2 fin-volume * tail-efficiency * fin-lift-slope * figures@fin-arm span / * to yaw-damping
	fin-volume tail-efficiency * fin-lift-slope * figures@rudder-share flap-effectiveness * rudder-travel * to rudder-power
	figures@mass 9.81 * area / to wing-loading
	figures@static-margin wing-loading * pitch-damping chord * 9.81 * 1.225 * 4 / - to pull-moment
	figures@stick-force-per-g elevator-power * pull-moment / to elevator-stiffness
	rudder-force full-rudder-speed sea-level-pressure / to rudder-stiffness
	figures
	{ :centre centre :dry-inertia dry-inertia :fuel-inertia fuel-inertia
	  :tail-slope tail-slope :wing-arm wing-arm :pitch-offset pitch-offset :pitch-damping pitch-damping
	  :elevator-power elevator-power :fin-stability fin-stability :fuselage-stability fuselage-yaw :yaw-damping yaw-damping :rudder-power rudder-power
	  :elevator-stiffness elevator-stiffness :rudder-stiffness rudder-stiffness
	  :reversal-pressure figures@reversal-speed sea-level-pressure
	  :panel-arm span 2 / 4 3 PI * / *
	  :tail-share figures@main-wheel 2 @i centre 2 @i - figures@main-wheel 2 @i figures@tail-wheel 2 @i - / } merge ;

\ the Spitfire's masses but the guns: the published Merlin III (1,335 lb), and estimates of the propeller, the radiator and coolant, the pilot with his parachute, the fuel tanks' 85 gallons full, the wing's structure spread over its span as an ellipse is, and the tail; each where the model in aircraft.telic puts it, the radiator under the wing (taken on the centre line), the wing's structure about its mid-chord line, 1.17 m forward of the origin
: airframe-parts ( -- components )
	[ 606 [ 0 0.1 2.3 ] point-mass
	  160 [ 0 0 3.6 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 -0.9 0.8 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 0.3 -0.2 ] point-mass
	  278 [ 0 0.2 1.1 ] fuel-mass-at
	  420 [ 0 -0.65 1.17 ] [ 2.81 0.1 0.6 ] false component
	  55 [ 0 0.4 -4.4 ] [ 0.6 0.4 0.3 ] false component ] ;

\ the eight Brownings, the published 21 lb 14 oz each, with an estimated 7.6 kg of their 300 rounds, where the model's gun ports are, reaching back 1 m from the leading edge
: gun-parts ( -- components )
	[ 1.9 2.15 2.9 3.15 ] ( out | [ out negate out ] ) map [ ] ' concat reduce
	( out | 17.52 [ out -0.6 1.8 ] point-mass ) map ;

\ the Mk I's centre of gravity's place along the model, metres forward of its origin: the published limits, 8.25 to 9.9 in aft of the datum (spitfireperformance.com's K5054 trials page), their middle 9.1 in; the datum at 23% of the mean aerodynamic chord (a forum thread on the Spitfire's controllability, theairtacticalassaultgroup.com); that chord, 2.165 m, its leading edge 4.8 in behind the root's (NACA's flying qualities report on the Spitfire VA); the root's leading edge where the model puts it, 2.33 m forward
2.33 0.122 - 0.23 2.165 * - 9.1 0.0254 * - constant spitfire-centre-along

\ the fuselage: the Mk I's loaded weight less every other mass, spread along its length, centred where it puts the whole aircraft's centre of gravity at its published place, the one mass whose place is not otherwise known
: fuselage-part ( -- component )
	airframe-parts gun-parts concat to named-parts
	named-parts ( part | part@mass ) map 0 ' + reduce to named
	named-parts ( part | part@place 2 @i part@mass * ) map 0 ' + reduce to named-moment
	2624 named - to remainder
	spitfire-centre-along 2624 * named-moment - remainder / to remainder-along
	remainder [ 0 0 remainder-along ] [ 0.35 0.4 1.88 ] false component ;

\ the Spitfire Mk I's masses
: spitfire-components ( -- components )
	airframe-parts gun-parts concat [ fuselage-part ] concat ;

\ the Spitfire Mk I's figures. Its rudder's share of the fin's chord, 0.31, measured at mid-height on the fin and hinge aircraft.telic draws. Its split flaps' 85° travel, and its ailerons' full total deflection, the two ailerons' angles added, about 41° right and 45° left, taken as 21.5° each (Measurements of the Flying Qualities of a Supermarine Spitfire VA Airplane, NACA, 1942, its figures 25 and 26). Its rudder trimming tab worked from a handwheel in the cockpit (the Air Ministry's modification to the Spitfire I's rudder trimmer tab's range, 17 August 1939; the Spitfire II's Pilot's Notes set it fully to starboard for take-off). Its stall as NACA's flight tests of a Spitfire VA found it (Stalling Characteristics of the Supermarine Spitfire VA Airplane, NACA WR L-353): good warning by buffeting, the nose easing down at the start of the stall and no violent motions even beyond the most lift; in a hard turn the stick could be pulled far back without losing control, the aircraft pitching down and recovering by itself when the stick was let go; with the gun ports open a right roll past about 10° of up elevator. Modelled, as estimates: the buffet from 4° below the stall angle, early enough for the report's good warning to be felt before a hard pull reaches the stall, a nose-down pitching moment past it, and the lift falling past it 1.2 for each radian, as the Hurricane's
: spitfire-mk-i-figures ( -- figures )
	{ :name "SPITFIRE MK I" :kind :spitfire :model-down :spitfire-down :finish :temperate-land
	  :rounds-per-gun 300 :fire-seconds 300 60 * browning-rate / :stick-force-per-g 5
	  :mass 2624 :span 11.23 :wing-area 22.48 :chord 2.165
	  :engine-power 768000 :full-throttle-height 5500 :fuel-capacity 85
	  :lift-slope 4.8 :zero-lift-coefficient 0.12 :stall-angle 0.26 :parasite-drag 0.019 :induced-drag 0.063
	  :static-thrust 10500 :propeller-efficiency 0.8
	  :dihedral 6 PI 180 / * :tailplane-area 2.89 :tail-arm 4.25 spitfire-centre-along + :fin-area 1.47 :fin-arm 4.6 spitfire-centre-along + :fin-along -4.6 :fin-root 0.33 :fin-tip 1.25
	  :static-margin 0.03 :trim-lift 0.25 :flap-moment -0.07 :flap-limit 140 :rudder-bias true :rudder-share 0.31 :reserve-capacity 0
	  :flap-travel 85 :aileron-travel 21.5
	  :main-wheel [ 0.865 -1.52 1.809 ] :wheel-radius 0.32 :tail-wheel [ 0 -0.55 -4.42 ] :tail-wheel-radius 0.1
	  :hub [ 0 0 3.62 ] :belly -0.8 :wing-root-up -0.70
	  :aileron-lift 0.32 :aileron-stiffness aileron-force 75.1 sea-level-pressure / :reversal-speed 214.6
	  :buffet-margin 4 PI 180 / * :post-stall-slope 1.2 :stall-pitch-down -0.5
	  :components spitfire-components } ;

\ the photographic reconnaissance Spitfire, the PR IV: the Merlin 46's published 1,415 hp at 14,000 ft; the leading edges' 66.5 gallons a side with the 85 behind the engine, 218 in all; no guns, the cameras in their place, an estimated 100 kg lighter for it; the leading edges' fuel, 1.8 m out and 0.3 m behind the leading edge, 2.0 m forward of the model's origin (estimates), a tenth more inertia in roll when full; the airframe's remainder the Mk I's, in the same place; painted PRU Blue overall, Type B roundels on its upper wings (published)
: pr-iv-figures ( -- figures )
	airframe-parts [ fuselage-part
	                 40 [ 0 0 -1.8 ] point-mass
	                 217.5 [ -1.8 -0.6 2.0 ] fuel-mass-at
	                 217.5 [ 1.8 -0.6 2.0 ] fuel-mass-at ] concat to parts
	spitfire-mk-i-figures
	{ :name "SPITFIRE PR IV" :finish :pru-blue :mass 2959 :engine-power 1055000 :full-throttle-height 4267 :fuel-capacity 218
	  :components parts } merge ;

\ the Hurricane Mk I's masses: the published Merlin III; estimates of the propeller, the radiator under the fuselage with its coolant, the pilot, the wing's structure (its thick wing heavier than the Spitfire's), the tail; the published 97 gallons, 34.5 in each tank between the centre section's spars and 28 in the reserve tank ahead of the cockpit; the eight Brownings, four in each wing outboard of the undercarriage, the inner pairs with the published 334 rounds each and the outer with 287 (an estimated 25.4 g a round with its link); the fuselage the remainder of the loaded weight. Each placed where the model as drawn from the M. L. Watts three-view puts it: the wing's root from 1.25 m ahead of the origin, the pilot under the hood
: hurricane-components ( -- components )
	[ 606 [ 0 0.15 1.9 ] point-mass
	  160 [ 0 0.05 3.05 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 -1.0 -0.5 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 0.55 -0.45 ] point-mass
	  112.8 [ -1.0 -0.3 0.7 ] fuel-mass-at
	  112.8 [ 1.0 -0.3 0.7 ] fuel-mass-at
	  91.6 [ 0 0.2 1.4 ] fuel-mass-at
	  540 [ 0 -0.4 0.1 ] [ 2.7 0.15 0.6 ] false component
	  70 [ 0 0.4 -5.3 ] [ 0.7 0.5 0.3 ] false component ] to parts
	[ [ 2.2 334 ] [ 2.4 334 ] [ 2.6 287 ] [ 2.8 287 ] ] ( gun |
		gun first to out
		gun second 0.0254 * 9.92 + to gun-mass
		[ gun-mass [ out negate -0.35 0.6 ] point-mass gun-mass [ out -0.35 0.6 ] point-mass ]
	) map [ ] ' concat reduce to guns
	parts guns concat to named
	2994 named ( part | part@mass ) map 0 ' + reduce - to remainder
	named [ remainder [ 0 0.1 -0.9 ] [ 0.4 0.5 2.0 ] false component ] concat ;

\ the Hurricane Mk I's figures. Its rudder's share of the fin's chord, 0.45, measured at mid-height on the fin and hinge aircraft.telic draws. Published: the flaps' 80° travel and the ailerons' 19.2° to 21.5° down and 20.6° to 22.4° up, taken as 20.5° either way (Nissen and Phillips, NACA MR, April 1942); the loaded weight, 6,600 lb (the-battle-of-britain.co.uk); the span, 40 ft, and wing area, 257.5 sq ft; the Merlin III; 97 gallons; the guns' rounds; the flaps' 120 mph limit, from the placard in the Smithsonian's Mk IIC cockpit; the rudder bias wheel and the fuel cock between the main tanks and the 28-gallon reserve, seen in that cockpit. Measured from the model as drawn from the M. L. Watts three-view: the wing's root, its chord's middle 0.62 m below the propeller's axis; the tailplane, 2.75 m², its quarter chord 5.1 m aft of the model's origin; the fin and rudder, 2.07 m² of the 2.39 m² outline standing clear of the fuselage, centred 5.48 m aft, from the fuselage's top there, level with the propeller's axis, to the fin's tip, 1.32 m above it; the arms, 5.34 m and 5.72 m, from the centre of gravity the masses give, 0.24 m ahead of the origin. Estimates: the lift slope, the thicker wing's higher aspect ratio's (Helmbold's formula, 3% above the Spitfire's); the induced drag, an efficiency of 0.8 for its tapered wing; the dihedral, 3.5°; the margin, 8% of the chord, more than the Spitfire's, as its steadiness as a gun platform was reported; the stick force, 8 lb a g; the ailerons the Spitfire's, with no published rate of roll found, and its thick wing's reversal speed 700 mph. The stall as NACA's flight tests of a Hurricane II, the Mk I's wing (Nissen and Phillips, NACA WR L-565, 1942), found it: never very violent, recovered by normal use of the controls; the flow breaking down first at the centre section, pitching the nose down, its buffeting warning the pilot while back stick remained before any roll; held stalled, a roll that stopped of its own accord. Modelled, as estimates: the buffet from 3° below the stall angle, a nose-down pitching moment past it, and the lift falling past it 1.2 for each radian. The parasite drag is set so the model reaches the A&AEE's 316 mph at 20,000 ft for L.2026 with the Rotol constant-speed propeller (June 1940)
: hurricane-mk-i-figures ( -- figures )
	{ :name "HURRICANE MK I" :kind :hurricane :model-down :hurricane-down :finish :temperate-land
	  :rounds-per-gun 310.5 :fire-seconds 310.5 60 * browning-rate / :stick-force-per-g 8
	  :mass 2994 :span 12.19 :wing-area 23.92 :chord 1.96
	  :engine-power 768000 :full-throttle-height 5500 :fuel-capacity 97
	  :lift-slope 4.96 :zero-lift-coefficient 0.12 :stall-angle 0.26 :parasite-drag 0.0253 :induced-drag 0.064
	  :static-thrust 10500 :propeller-efficiency 0.8
	  :dihedral 3.5 PI 180 / * :tailplane-area 2.75 :tail-arm 5.34 :fin-area 2.07 :fin-arm 5.72 :fin-along -5.48 :fin-root 0.05 :fin-tip 1.37
	  :static-margin 0.08 :trim-lift 0.25 :flap-moment -0.07 :flap-limit 120 :rudder-bias true :rudder-share 0.45 :reserve-capacity 28
	  :flap-travel 80 :aileron-travel 20.5
	  :main-wheel [ 1.195 -1.74 0.97 ] :wheel-radius 0.35 :tail-wheel [ 0 -0.84 -4.76 ] :tail-wheel-radius 0.1
	  :hub [ 0 0.05 3.05 ] :belly -0.82 :wing-root-up -0.62
	  :aileron-lift 0.32 :aileron-stiffness aileron-force 75.1 sea-level-pressure / :reversal-speed 313
	  :buffet-margin 3 PI 180 / * :post-stall-slope 1.2 :stall-pitch-down -0.5
	  :components hurricane-components } ;

\ the Lysander Mk II's centre of gravity's place along the model, metres forward of its origin: 30% of the chord back from the leading edge where the wing is widest, 3 m out, its leading edge 0.49 m forward and its chord 1.94 m (an estimate; no published limits found)
0.49 0.3 1.94 * - constant lysander-centre-along

\ the Lysander Mk II's masses: the published Perseus XII, 1,025 lb; estimates of the de Havilland propeller, the pilot and the gunner with their parachutes, the wing's structure spread over its span about its mid-chord, the undercarriage's legs, fairings and wheels, the tail; the published 95 gallons in the tank behind the pilot, under the wing (its place an estimate); the two Brownings in the wheels' fairings, 21 lb 14 oz each with an estimated 500 rounds of 25.4 g; the fuselage the remainder of the loaded weight, placed where it puts the whole aircraft's centre of gravity at its estimated place. Each placed where the model as drawn from the three-view puts it
: lysander-components ( -- components )
	[ 465 [ 0 0.1 1.6 ] point-mass
	  110 [ 0 0.1 2.23 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 0.7 -0.1 ] point-mass
	  90 [ 0 0.7 -1.9 ] point-mass
	  310.6 [ 0 0.9 -0.9 ] fuel-mass-at
	  380 [ 0 1.25 -0.5 ] [ 3.8 0.15 0.5 ] false component
	  180 [ 0 -1.3 0.45 ] [ 1.2 0.4 0.3 ] false component
	  70 [ 0 0.3 -5.6 ] [ 0.9 0.6 0.3 ] false component
	  22.6 [ -1.4 -1.4 0.9 ] point-mass
	  22.6 [ 1.4 -1.4 0.9 ] point-mass ] to named
	named ( part | part@mass ) map 0 ' + reduce to named-mass
	named ( part | part@place 2 @i part@mass * ) map 0 ' + reduce to named-moment
	2728 named-mass - to remainder
	lysander-centre-along 2728 * named-moment - remainder / to remainder-along
	named [ remainder [ 0 0.2 remainder-along ] [ 0.45 0.5 2.2 ] false component ] concat ;

\ the Lysander Mk II's figures. Published: the loaded weight, 6,015 lb (armedconflicts.com's Mk II page); the span, 50 ft, and wing area, 260 sq ft; the Bristol Perseus XII, air-cooled, 905 hp at 2,750 rpm at its rated height of 6,500 ft, its 0.5 reduction gear (Wikipedia's Bristol Perseus), turning anticlockwise seen from the cockpit as Bristol's radials are listed, the Mercury it was developed from a left-hand tractor (Wikipedia's Bristol Mercury) and the Pegasus anticlockwise seen from behind (enginehistory.org's Pegasus II), the Perseus's own direction not found stated; 95 gallons; the stall near 65 mph with the automatic slats open and the flaps they work down. Measured on the model as drawn from the three-view: the propeller's radius, 1.59 m; the wing's root, its chord's middle 1.13 m above the propeller's axis; the tailplane, 4.7 m² of the 5.19 m² outline clear of the fuselage, its quarter chord 5.34 m aft of the origin; the fin and rudder, 2.52 m² standing clear of the fuselage, centred 5.56 m aft, from the fuselage's top there, 0.52 m up, to the fin's tip, 2.20 m up, the rudder 0.37 of the chord at mid-height; the arms from the centre of gravity's estimated place. Estimates: the lift slope, by Helmbold's formula for the wing's 9.6 aspect ratio; the induced drag, an efficiency of 0.8; the slotted flaps' added lift, 0.45, and the stall angle at which, with them down, the lift reaches the 65 mph stall's 2.14; the slats opening from 8° and fully open at 14°, drawing the flaps down with them, as the Lysander's slats worked its flaps; the dihedral, 4°, the gull wing's 2° weighted by area with a high wing's effect; the margin, 8% of the chord; the stick force, 10 lb a g; the variable-incidence tailplane, worked by its wheel, holding 0.8 of the elevator's travel and wound through its whole range in 20 s, the many turns of its large crank, where the fighters' trim tabs take 5 s; the two-pitch propeller's 2,450 rpm fine and 2,300 coarse at full throttle standing still, coarse reaching the rated 2,750 near the top speed, fine pitch over-revving past the 2,650 rpm limit as speed builds, as a Lysander pilot's account describes; the rated boost, +3 lb, with no cut-out; the fuel burned at full power, 75 gallons an hour, from an assumed 0.6 lb a horsepower-hour; the cylinder heads' heating, their quicker response than a glycol system's and their limit of 260°C, seizing at 300°C, the oil's temperature 0.35 of theirs; the static thrust in proportion to the Spitfire's by power; the ailerons the Spitfire's, rigged 0.06 of their travel left, the setting at which the model flies hands-off at full throttle at 1,000 m, as a rigger's tab would be set, the wing roots at the slipstream's top taking back only a fifth of the torque's roll; the stall's buffet and pitch-down as the Hurricane's. The parasite drag is set so the model reaches the published 230 mph at 5,000 ft
