\ the Gladiator Mk II's centre of gravity's place along the model, metres forward of its origin, the origin 2.40 m behind the propeller's hub: 30% of the cellule's mean chord behind its leading edge, the upper wing's leading edge 1.51 m forward and the lower's 0.61 m, both chords 1.68 m (an estimate; no published limits found)
1.06 0.3 1.68 * - constant gladiator-centre-along

\ the Gladiator Mk II's masses: the Mercury VIII, 966 lb being the Mercury VI-S's dry weight and the VIII a lightened engine (Wikipedia's Bristol Mercury), taken as 440 kg; estimates of the Fairey-Reed metal propeller, the pilot with his parachute, the upper wing's structure spread over its span, the lower's, the undercarriage's cantilever legs and wheels, the tail; 85 gallons in the fuselage tank ahead of the pilot (unconfirmed); the four Brownings, 21 lb 14 oz each, two in the fuselage with an estimated 600 rounds and two under the lower wing with 400, at 25.4 g a round; the fuselage the remainder of the loaded weight, placed where it puts the whole aircraft's centre of gravity at its estimated place. Each placed where the three-view puts it
