\ the Gladiator Mk II's figures. Published: the loaded weight, 4,864 lb; the span, 32 ft 3 in, both wings, and the wing area, 323 sq ft; the length, 27 ft 5 in; the Bristol Mercury VIII, air-cooled, 825 hp at 2,750 rpm, a left-hand tractor as the Mercury is listed (Wikipedia's Bristol Mercury), with the gun synchronising gear of the VIIIA; the three-blade fixed-pitch Fairey-Reed propeller of 10 ft 6 in; 257 mph at 14,600 ft; four Brownings. Measured on the three-view (the-blueprints.com's Gladiator, the plan and rear views at 84 px/m from the span and the side view at 117 px/m from the length): both wings' chord 1.68 m, the upper wing 1.25 m above the thrust line with its leading edge 1.51 m forward of the origin, the lower 0.45 m below it staggered 0.90 m back, a gap of 1.7 m; the tailplane, 4.3 m² clear of the fuselage, its quarter chord 4.15 m aft; the fin and rudder, 2.0 m², centred 4.9 m aft, from the fuselage's top there, 0.45 m up, to the fin's tip, 1.46 m up; the main wheels 0.98 m out, their axles 1.77 m below the thrust line and 1.13 m forward of the origin, the tail wheel 4.14 m aft. Estimates: the two wings as one of their joint area, its lift slope cut to 4.2 by the biplane's interference and its induced drag raised for it; the parasite drag of the airframe over the fixed undercarriage's own, which the model adds, and the propeller's efficiency, set together so the model reaches 257 mph at 4,450 m (421 km/h against the published 414) and climbs 742 m/min against the published 788; the dihedral, 2°; the margin, 6% of the chord; the stick force, 6 lb a g; the stall angle and the lift past it as the fighters'; the fixed-pitch propeller as a two-pitch one whose settings are alike, 2,200 rpm at full throttle standing; the rated boost, +2 lb, with no cut-out; the fuel burned at full power, 69 gallons an hour, from an assumed 0.6 lb a horsepower-hour; the cylinder heads' heating and limits as the Lysander's; split flaps on all four wings of a lesser lift, limited to 110 mph; no rudder trim
